<BaseLayout pageTitle="About Me">
	<p> 
		I am an EECS PhD student at the University of California Irvine, where I do research in
		high performance computing and AI for Science. I earned my B.S. in computer science from Trinity University.
	</p>
	<p>
		Part of my current research is builing simulations and ML-based surrogates for two-phase liquid cooling (boiling).
		Boiling water is the most efficient known way to do heat transfer (cooling) for a lot of applications, such as
		cooling high-rack density data centers or nuclear energy systems.
		A lot of my research has focused on low order models (Essentially meaning they're cheap, but inaccurate.)
		that can be used for design optimization or prototyping.
	</p>
	<p>
		I also have a lot of experience with software systems and parallel programming.
	</p>
</BaseLayout>